  {
    "id": "corrosion", "title": "Corrosion Control", "track": "general", "icon": "&#x1F9EA;",
    "sections": [
      {"heading": "What Corrosion Is", "body": "Electrochemical deterioration of metal returning it toward its ore state. Needs an <b>anode, cathode, electrolyte, and path</b>. Aluminum forms a protective oxide; chlorides (salt) and moisture break it down. Costs airlines billions and grounds aircraft."},
      {"heading": "Types", "body": "<ul><li><b>Surface/uniform</b> - general etching</li><li><b>Pitting</b> - localized deep holes</li><li><b>Intergranular</b> - along grain boundaries (heat-treat related)</li><li><b>Exfoliation</b> - layers lift/flake</li><li><b>Galvanic</b> - dissimilar metals in contact</li><li><b>Stress corrosion cracking</b>, <b>filiform</b>, <b>fretting</b></li></ul>"},
      {"heading": "Prevention", "body": "Protective finishes: <b>alodine/chromate conversion</b>, primer, paint. <b>Anodizing</b> aluminum. Seal faying surfaces. Drainage/ventilation. Keep it clean and dry. Corrosion-preventive compounds (CPC/LPS). Dissimilar-metal isolation (barrier tape, sealant)."},
      {"heading": "Treatment", "body": "Remove all corrosion products (mechanical/chemical), assess to damage limits per SRM, neutralize, restore protective finish. Non-powered abrasives on aluminum (no steel wool - it embeds iron). Document; re-inspect on schedule."},
      {"heading": "Corrosion Inspection in Practice", "body": "During a scheduled inspection, look first at known trouble spots: battery compartments, lavatory/galley floor areas, wheel wells, exhaust trails, and skin lap joints where moisture pools. A bright white or gray powdery bloom on aluminum is a classic sign of corrosion starting under paint. Use a flashlight at a raking angle and a mirror to check blind areas; tap-test suspect skin for disbonding. Any find gets photographed, measured, and compared against SRM allowable damage limits before any blend-out or repair is approved."}
    , {"heading": "Corrosion in Composite-to-Metal Interfaces", "body": "Galvanic corrosion is a particular concern where carbon fiber composites contact aluminum structure, since carbon fiber is electrically conductive and more noble than aluminum, creating a strong galvanic couple in the presence of moisture. Isolation is achieved using fiberglass isolation plies, non-conductive fasteners or sleeves, and sealant barriers between the composite and metal structure. Any breach of this isolation layer during repair or drilling can introduce accelerated corrosion at the interface that may not be visible until significant material loss has occurred, making proper isolation restoration during composite repairs a critical inspection item."}, {"heading": "Corrosion Removal Limits and Blend-Out Procedures", "body": "When corrosion is found on a structural part, the repair manual specifies allowable removal limits, typically expressed as a percentage of material thickness that may be blended out before the part is considered damaged beyond repairable limits. Blending involves removing corrosion products and a small amount of sound material using abrasive methods to create a smooth, feathered transition that avoids stress risers, followed by thickness measurement (often with an ultrasonic thickness gauge) to confirm remaining material meets minimum requirements. If blend-out would exceed the allowable limit, the part must be repaired using an approved doubler or patch, or replaced entirely. After blending, the area must be re-treated with the appropriate corrosion-preventive coating or conversion coating before paint or primer is reapplied, since bare blended metal is highly susceptible to renewed corrosion."}, {"heading": "Corrosion Inhibiting Compounds (CICs) and Their Application", "body": "Corrosion inhibiting compounds (CICs) are specialized products applied to aircraft structure, particularly in areas prone to moisture retention like lap joints, wheel wells, and lower fuselage structure, to displace moisture and leave a protective film that inhibits corrosion initiation without requiring full disassembly and repainting. CICs vary in properties such as viscosity, dry-to-touch time, and creeping ability (the tendency to migrate into tight crevices and faying surfaces through capillary action), and selecting the appropriate CIC for a specific application requires matching these properties to the location and purpose, since a CIC formulated for heavy structure protection may not adequately penetrate tight lap joint crevices where corrosion often initiates unseen. Application intervals and locations for CIC treatment are specified in the operator's corrosion prevention and control program, often more frequent in aircraft operating in corrosive environments like coastal or high-humidity regions, and mechanics must follow the specified reapplication schedule rather than assuming a single application provides indefinite protection. CIC application must avoid areas where it could interfere with other systems, such as certain electrical connectors, static wicks, or areas requiring specific surface friction for tire/brake function, since these compounds can migrate beyond their intended application area if not carefully controlled during application."},
    {"heading": "Exfoliation Corrosion in Extruded Aluminum Structures", "body": "Exfoliation corrosion is a severe form of intergranular corrosion that occurs in extruded and some forged high-strength aluminum alloys, where corrosion product forms along elongated grain boundaries parallel to the extrusion direction, causing the material to swell and separate into layers like the pages of a book. Because the corrosion propagates along internal grain boundaries, exfoliation can be far more advanced internally than surface appearance suggests, and by the time surface blistering or flaking becomes visible, significant structural cross-section may already be lost. Extruded parts such as stringers, longerons, and window/door frames are particularly susceptible, and inspection must include awareness of the specific alloy's exfoliation susceptibility, careful attention to any surface roughening or blistering along the extrusion's length, and eddy current or ultrasonic inspection where exfoliation is suspected, since visual inspection alone can significantly underestimate the extent of damage."}],
    "quiz": [ {"q": "Why is a CIC's 'creeping ability' (capillary migration into tight crevices) an important property for treating lap joints?", "choices": ["Creeping ability is irrelevant to CIC effectiveness", "Lap joint corrosion often initiates in tight faying surface crevices that require a CIC capable of migrating into these tight spaces to provide protection", "CICs with high creeping ability should never be used on aircraft", "Lap joints do not require corrosion protection"], "answer": 1}, {"q": "After blending out corrosion on a structural skin panel, what must be verified before the part can be returned to service?", "choices": ["The part's color matches surrounding structure", "Remaining material thickness meets the manual's minimum allowable limit", "The part is heavier than before", "No further action is needed once corrosion is visually gone"], "answer": 1}, {"q": "Why is carbon fiber composite in contact with aluminum a galvanic corrosion concern?", "choices": ["Carbon fiber is an insulator", "Carbon fiber is conductive and more noble than aluminum, creating a strong galvanic couple", "Aluminum is more noble than carbon fiber", "Composites never touch metal in aircraft"], "answer": 1, "explain": "Carbon fiber's conductivity and nobility relative to aluminum create a strong galvanic couple, requiring isolation plies or sealant barriers to prevent accelerated corrosion."},
      {"q": "During inspection, a raking-light/mirror technique is used mainly to:", "choices": ["Measure paint thickness", "Reveal corrosion bloom or disbonding in blind/shadowed areas", "Check fuel quantity", "Test bonding straps"], "answer": 1, "explain": "Raking light and mirrors reveal subtle surface irregularities like corrosion blooms or skin disbonding that are easy to miss under normal lighting."},
      {"q": "Corrosion requires an electrolyte plus:", "choices": ["Anode, cathode, and path","Only heat","Only oxygen","Sunlight"], "answer": 0, "explain": "An electrochemical cell needs anode, cathode, electrolyte, and a conductive path."},
      {"q": "Layers lifting/flaking describes:", "choices": ["Pitting","Exfoliation","Filiform","Fretting"], "answer": 1, "explain": "Exfoliation corrosion lifts material in layers, common in extrusions."},
      {"q": "Dissimilar metals in contact cause:", "choices": ["Galvanic corrosion","Fatigue","Creep","Annealing"], "answer": 0, "explain": "Galvanic corrosion occurs between dissimilar metals with an electrolyte present."},
      {"q": "Why avoid steel wool on aluminum?", "choices": ["Too soft","Embeds iron particles that promote corrosion","It's expensive","It scratches paint only"], "answer": 1, "explain": "Steel wool leaves iron particles that create galvanic cells and accelerate corrosion."},
      {"q": "Chromate conversion coating (alodine) provides:", "choices": ["Structural strength","Corrosion protection and paint adhesion","Fuel resistance","Fire protection"], "answer": 1, "explain": "It forms a corrosion-resistant film and improves paint/primer adhesion."},
    {"q": "Why can exfoliation corrosion be more structurally significant than its surface appearance suggests?", "choices": ["Exfoliation corrosion only ever affects the very outer surface layer", "The corrosion propagates along internal grain boundaries, so significant structural cross-section may be lost before surface blistering becomes visible", "Exfoliation corrosion is purely cosmetic and never affects structural integrity", "It only occurs on non-structural fairings and covers"], "answer": 1}
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "human_factors", "title": "Human Factors & Safety", "track": "general", "icon": "&#x1F9E0;",
    "sections": [
      {"heading": "The Dirty Dozen", "body": "The 12 human-factors precursors to maintenance error: <b>Lack of communication, complacency, lack of knowledge, distraction, lack of teamwork, fatigue, lack of resources, pressure, lack of assertiveness, stress, lack of awareness, norms.</b> Recognizing them is the first defense."},
      {"heading": "SHELL & Error Chains", "body": "The <b>SHELL model</b>: Software, Hardware, Environment, Liveware (people) - errors happen at interfaces. Most accidents are a <b>chain</b> of small failures; breaking one link prevents the event. Swiss-cheese model of layered defenses."},
      {"heading": "Safety Nets", "body": "<b>Checklists</b>, work-cards, and sign-offs. Independent inspection of critical items (flight controls). <b>Fatigue management</b> - shift limits. <b>Just culture</b> - report errors without blame to learn. Proper documentation prevents repeat mistakes."},
      {"heading": "Shop Safety & PPE", "body": "Eye/hearing/respiratory protection. <b>Lockout/tagout</b> for energized systems. SDS/HazMat handling. Compressed-gas and battery-acid precautions. Confined-space (fuel tank) entry procedures. Proper lifting and FOD discipline."}
    ,
      {"heading": "Applying Human Factors Day to Day", "body": "A short <b>pre-task risk brief</b> - reviewing the job, hazards, and required tools/parts before starting - catches many errors before they happen. <b>Independent inspection</b> on flight-critical items (like control cable rigging) adds a second set of eyes. When interrupted mid-task, always re-verify the last completed step before continuing rather than assuming where you left off - interruption-driven errors are one of the most common causes of maintenance mistakes."}, {"heading": "Fatigue Risk Management", "body": "Fatigue impairs judgment, reaction time, and attention to detail similarly to alcohol intoxication, and maintenance fatigue risk management systems address scheduling practices, rest requirements, and self-assessment tools to reduce fatigue-related errors. Circadian rhythm disruption from shift work, especially rotating shifts and night shifts, is a major contributor, and technicians should recognize warning signs such as microsleeps, repeated re-reading of instructions, and difficulty concentrating as cues to pause and seek relief rather than continuing to work through fatigue. Organizations with mature safety cultures build fatigue reporting into their non-punitive safety reporting systems alongside other human factors hazards."}, {"heading": "Just Culture and Reporting Systems (ASAP/ASRS)", "body": "A 'just culture' balances individual accountability with a non-punitive environment that encourages reporting of errors and hazards before they cause accidents. Programs like the Aviation Safety Action Program (ASAP) and the Aviation Safety Reporting System (ASRS) allow mechanics to voluntarily report safety concerns, including their own errors, without fear of certificate action in most cases, provided the report is filed promptly and the event was not intentional or criminal. These systems generate valuable data used to identify systemic issues, such as recurring tooling problems or procedural ambiguities, that individual blame would never surface. A healthy just culture distinguishes between honest mistakes, at-risk behavior, and reckless conduct, applying different responses to each rather than treating every error the same way. Understanding these systems helps new mechanics see error reporting as a professional responsibility rather than a personal risk."}, {"heading": "Communication Breakdowns and Shift Turnover Hazards", "body": "Shift turnover, when one maintenance crew hands off in-progress work to another crew, represents a well-documented high-risk point for communication breakdown, since critical information about work status, open discrepancies, or incomplete tasks can be lost, misunderstood, or simply not communicated if a structured handoff process is not followed. Effective shift turnover procedures typically require a written or documented handoff, not just a verbal conversation, since verbal-only handoffs are vulnerable to incomplete information transfer, especially during busy or noisy shift-change periods, and documentation provides a reviewable record if a question arises later about what was or was not communicated. Standardized turnover checklists or logs that explicitly capture aircraft status, open work items, safety-critical incomplete steps (such as a partially reassembled system or removed access panel), and any unusual observations help ensure the incoming crew has a complete picture rather than relying on the outgoing crew's memory of what seemed important to mention. Several documented aviation maintenance incidents have been traced directly to incomplete or informal shift turnover communication, reinforcing why structured handoff discipline is treated as a critical human factors safety practice rather than a mere administrative formality."},
    {"heading": "Complacency and Normalization of Deviance", "body": "Complacency develops when a technician performs a familiar task so many times without incident that attention and vigilance quietly decrease, leading to skipped steps or reduced scrutiny even though the task's risk has not actually changed. Normalization of deviance is a related but distinct phenomenon where a minor deviation from a procedure or standard is tolerated once without consequence, then repeated and gradually accepted as the new normal, until an entire team or organization operates with a drifted standard that would have been recognized as unacceptable if introduced all at once. Both hazards are countered by treating checklists and procedures as literal steps to be followed and verified every time regardless of familiarity, by encouraging reporting of near-misses and small deviations before they become normalized, and by periodic fresh-eyes review (a second person, a different shift, or a formal audit) that is more likely to notice a drifted standard than someone immersed in it daily."}],
    "quiz": [ {"q": "Why is a purely verbal shift turnover considered a human factors risk in aviation maintenance?", "choices": ["Verbal communication is always more reliable than written documentation", "Verbal-only handoffs are vulnerable to incomplete information transfer, especially during busy periods, and lack a reviewable record", "Shift turnover has no relationship to maintenance safety", "Written handoff documentation is never necessary"], "answer": 1}, {"q": "What is the primary purpose of voluntary safety reporting programs like ASAP and ASRS in a just culture?", "choices": ["To punish mechanics who make mistakes", "To collect data on hazards and errors so systemic issues can be corrected before accidents occur", "To replace all other safety training", "To reduce the number of inspections required"], "answer": 1}, {"q": "How does significant fatigue affect a technician's performance?", "choices": ["It has no measurable effect", "It impairs judgment and reaction time similarly to alcohol intoxication", "It only affects physical strength", "It improves attention to detail"], "answer": 1, "explain": "Research shows fatigue produces cognitive impairment comparable to alcohol intoxication, affecting judgment, reaction time, and attention to detail."},
      {"q": "How many items in the Dirty Dozen?", "choices": ["10","12","14","6"], "answer": 1, "explain": "The Dirty Dozen is 12 human-factors error precursors."},
      {"q": "The SHELL model's second L is:", "choices": ["Logic","Liveware (people)","Loading","Lift"], "answer": 1, "explain": "SHELL = Software, Hardware, Environment, Liveware - the human element."},
      {"q": "A just culture encourages:", "choices": ["Hiding mistakes","Reporting errors to learn without blame","Punishing all errors","Ignoring reports"], "answer": 1, "explain": "Just culture invites honest error reporting so the system can improve."},
      {"q": "The Swiss-cheese model describes:", "choices": ["Cheese storage","Layered defenses with holes that can align","Fuel filtering","A rivet pattern"], "answer": 1, "explain": "Accidents occur when holes in successive defensive layers align."},
      {"q": "After being interrupted in the middle of a task, a mechanic should:", "choices": ["Continue from memory","Re-verify the last completed step before continuing","Restart the whole job from scratch every time","Ask someone else to guess where they left off"], "answer": 1, "explain": "Interruptions are a leading cause of maintenance error; re-checking the last completed step before resuming prevents skipped or duplicated actions."},
      {"q": "Lockout/tagout is used to:", "choices": ["Label parts","Prevent accidental energizing of systems","Track tools","Log hours"], "answer": 1, "explain": "LOTO isolates and secures energy sources so a system can't be activated during work."},
    {"q": "How does normalization of deviance differ from ordinary complacency in a maintenance environment?", "choices": ["They are identical phenomena with no meaningful distinction", "Normalization of deviance involves a minor deviation being repeated and gradually accepted as the new normal, while complacency is reduced vigilance on a familiar task performed correctly", "Normalization of deviance only applies to individuals, never to teams or organizations", "Complacency is more dangerous than normalization of deviance in all cases"], "answer": 1}
    ]
  }
